Reverse engineer the following algorithm and explain its function in one sentence: def sort_numbers(arr):
    for i in range(len(arr)): 
        for j in range(i): 
            if arr[i] < arr[j]: 
                arr[i], arr[j] = arr[j], arr[i]
    return arr

To optimize the algorithm to handle duplicate values while maintaining their original order, we can modify the inner for-loop to start at index i instead of 0. This change ensures that when two elements have the same value, their relative order in the input array is preserved in the sorted array. Here is the modified inner for-loop:

```
for j in range(i, 0, -1):
    if arr[j] < arr[j-1]:
        arr[j], arr[j-1] = arr[j-1], arr[j]
    else:
        break
```

This modified algorithm is called Insertion Sort, and it works by inserting each element in its correct position in the already sorted part of the array. It has an average time complexity of O(n^2), but it performs better than the above algorithm when the input array is nearly sorted.

